-- ============================================================================
-- ROLLBACK for 20261106160400_sec_automation_revoke_scheduler_execute.sql
-- ============================================================================
-- *** APPLYING THIS FILE REOPENS A CONFIRMED CROSS-TENANT EXECUTION PATH. ***
--
-- It re-grants EXECUTE on execute_single_automation_rule and
-- evaluate_pending_rules to `authenticated`, which means:
--
--   * p_trigger_source := 'scheduler' is reachable from a browser again and
--     skips the only authorization check those functions have, and
--   * any logged-in user can again run evaluate_pending_rules, which sweeps
--     due rules across EVERY organization. Measured 2026-11-06: an org-B
--     user's call processed an org-A rule.
--
-- YOU ALMOST CERTAINLY DO NOT NEED THIS FILE.
--
-- The only thing 160400 can break is an application that still calls the two
-- revoked functions directly. If the WorkflowsPage.tsx deploy has to be rolled
-- back, roll THAT back and leave the revoke in place only if the reverted
-- frontend does not call them. If the reverted frontend DOES call
-- execute_single_automation_rule, the "Run rule" button will fail with 42501.
--
-- PREFER THIS instead of re-granting: leave 160400 applied and ship a frontend
-- that calls run_workflow_rule(uuid), which 20261106160200 created and which
-- is safe. A broken button is recoverable in one deploy; a reopened
-- cross-tenant execution path is not recoverable at all, because you cannot
-- know afterwards whether it was used.
--
-- If you apply this anyway, treat the exposure as live and re-apply 160400 as
-- soon as the frontend is fixed.
-- ============================================================================

GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.execute_single_automation_rule(uuid, uuid, text) TO authenticated;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.evaluate_pending_rules() TO authenticated;

-- `anon` is deliberately NOT re-granted. It held no EXECUTE on either function
-- before 160400 (measured: anon_can_execute = false), so granting it would
-- widen access past the original state rather than restore it.
